NNAAMMEE
     sskkeeyyiinniitt — change password or add user to S/Key authentication system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sskkeeyyiinniitt [--DDEErrssxx] [--aa _a_u_t_h_-_t_y_p_e] [--nn _c_o_u_n_t] [--mmdd55 | --rrmmdd116600 | --sshhaa11]
              [_u_s_e_r]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     sskkeeyyiinniitt initializes the system so you can use S/Key one-time passwords
     to log in.  The program will ask you to enter a secret passphrase which
     is used by skey(1) to generate one-time passwords: enter a phrase of sev‐
     eral words in response.  After the S/Key database has been updated, you
     can log in using either your regular password or using S/Key one-time
     passwords.

     sskkeeyyiinniitt requires you to type a secret passphrase, so it should be used
     only on a secure terminal.  For example, on the console of a workstation
     or over an encrypted network session.  If you are using sskkeeyyiinniitt while
     logged in over an untrusted network, follow the instructions given below
     with the --ss option.

     Before initializing an S/Key entry, the user must authenticate using ei‐
     ther a standard password or an S/Key challenge.  To use a one-time pass‐
     word for initial authentication, sskkeeyyiinniitt --aa sskkeeyy can be used.  The user
     will then be presented with the standard S/Key challenge and allowed to
     proceed if it is correct.

     sskkeeyyiinniitt prints a sequence number and a one-time password.  This password
     can't be used to log in; one-time passwords should be generated using
     skey(1) first.  The one-time password printed by sskkeeyyiinniitt can be used to
     verify if the right passphrase has been given to skey(1).  The one-time
     password with the corresponding sequence number printed by skey(1) should
     match the one printed by sskkeeyyiinniitt.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa _a_u_t_h_-_t_y_p_e
             Before an S/Key entry can be initialised, the user must authenti‐
             cate themselves to the system.  This option allows the authenti‐
             cation type to be specified, such as “passwd” or “skey”.

     --DD      Disables access to the S/Key database.  Only the superuser may
             use the --DD option.

     --EE      Enables access to the S/Key database.  Only the superuser may use
             the --EE option.

     --mmdd55 | --rrmmdd116600 | --sshhaa11
             Selects the hash algorithm: MD5, RMD-160 (160-bit Ripe Message
             Digest), or SHA1 (NIST Secure Hash Algorithm Revision 1).

     --nn _c_o_u_n_t
             Start the sskkeeyy sequence at _c_o_u_n_t (default is 100).

     --rr      Removes the user's S/Key entry.

     --ss      Secure mode.  The user is expected to have already used a secure
             machine to generate the first one-time password.  Without the --ss
             option the system will assume you are directly connected over se‐
             cure communications and prompt you for your secret passphrase.
             The --ss option also allows one to set the seed and count for com‐
             plete control of the parameters.

             When the --ss option is specified, sskkeeyyiinniitt will try to authenti‐
             cate the user via S/Key, instead of the default listed in
             _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_g_i_n_._c_o_n_f.  If a user has no entry in the S/Key database,
             an alternate authentication type must be specified via the --aa op‐
             tion (see above).  Entering a password or passphrase in plain
             text defeats the purpose of using “secure” mode.

             You can use sskkeeyyiinniitt --ss in combination with the sskkeeyy command to
             set the seed and count if you do not like the defaults.  To do
             this run sskkeeyyiinniitt --ss in one window and put in your count and
             seed, then run skey(1) in another window to generate the correct
             6 English words for that count and seed.  You can then "cut-and-
             paste" or type the words into the sskkeeyyiinniitt window.

     --xx      Displays one-time passwords in hexadecimal instead of ASCII.

     _u_s_e_r    The username to be changed/added.  By default the current user is
             operated on.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/login.conf  file containing authentication types
     /etc/skey        directory containing user entries for S/Key

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     $ skeyinit
     Password: <enter your regular password here>
     [Updating user with md5]
     Old seed: [md5] host12377
     Enter new secret passphrase: <type a new passphrase here>
     Again secret passphrase: <again>
     ID user skey is otp-md5 100 host12378
     Next login password: CITE BREW IDLE CAIN ROD DOME
     $ otp-md5 -n 3 100 host12378
     Enter secret passphrase: <type your passphrase here>
     98: WERE TUG EDDY GEAR GILL TEE
     99: NEAR HA TILT FIN LONG SNOW
     100: CITE BREW IDLE CAIN ROD DOME

     The one-time password for the next login will have sequence number 99.

